# RegulAIt — the routes every public front shares (ADR-0202 lane H, ADR-0203 lane T).
#
# Imported by the two public fronts, so there is exactly one routing path into
# the stack whichever way a request arrives:
#
#   ./Caddyfile         the `tls` / `hosted` front: Caddy terminates TLS itself
#                       (Let's Encrypt) on 80/443.
#   ./tunnel.Caddyfile  the `tunnel` front: Cloudflare terminates TLS at its edge
#                       and `cloudflared` carries the request over an outbound-only
#                       tunnel to Caddy on the compose network, as plain HTTP.
#
# Neither front writes its own routes. Add or change a route HERE, never in one
# front only, or the two public doors drift apart.
#
# Usage, inside a site block:
#
#   import routes.Caddyfile                         (once, at file top level)
#   import regulait_headers
#   import regulait_routes <client-ip> <proto>
#
#   <client-ip>  the address the gateway and Keycloak record as the caller. The
#                TLS front passes {remote_host} (the socket peer: it IS the
#                client). The tunnel front passes Cloudflare's CF-Connecting-IP,
#                and only after it has checked that the peer is its own
#                cloudflared container (tunnel.Caddyfile).
#   <proto>      what X-Forwarded-Proto says. Both fronts pass the literal
#                `https`: the TLS front serves nothing but HTTPS (port 80 only
#                redirects), and the tunnel front redirects anything Cloudflare
#                did not receive over HTTPS before it gets here.
#
# Every forwarding header is OVERWRITTEN, never appended or passed through, so
# nothing a client sends can choose the IP on the audit trail, the scheme the
# gateway's requestIsSecure() sees, or the host Keycloak builds links from. The
# gateway and Keycloak trust these headers only from the fronts' fixed address
# 172.28.0.2 (REGULAIT_TRUSTED_PROXIES, KC_PROXY_TRUSTED_ADDRESSES; ADR-0031).

(regulait_headers) {
	# HSTS is deliberately absent: the gateway owns it (REGULAIT_HSTS, ADR-0029
	# amendment). See the comment above `import regulait_headers` in ./Caddyfile.
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
		# Don't advertise the proxy version.
		-Server
	}
}

(regulait_routes) {
	# --- sign-in service (ADR-0202 lane H) --------------------------------------
	# Keycloak (compose profile `sso`) is published on THIS name under /idp, so a
	# hosted deployment needs one DNS record and one certificate. Only the public
	# surface is proxied: /idp/realms/ (OIDC endpoints, login pages) and
	# /idp/resources/ (their static assets). Everything else under /idp (the admin
	# console, /idp/admin/, metrics, health) is refused here, as
	# docs/deployment/SSO_KEYCLOAK.md § Hardening requires. Keycloak must run
	# with KC_HTTP_RELATIVE_PATH=/idp and KC_HOSTNAME=https://<host>/idp
	# (docs/deployment/HOSTED_HTTPS.md). Without the `sso` profile these paths
	# answer 502; nothing else changes.
	@idp_public path /idp/realms/* /idp/resources/*
	handle @idp_public {
		reverse_proxy {$REGULAIT_KC_UPSTREAM:keycloak:8080} {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {args[0]}
			header_up X-Real-IP {args[0]}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {args[1]}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
			# Only the tunnel front may read this header, and it does so before
			# this point. Nothing behind Caddy is allowed to see it.
			header_up -CF-Connecting-IP
		}
	}
	handle /idp* {
		respond 404
	}

	# --- reverse proxy ---------------------------------------------------------
	handle {
		# `gateway:3000` is the compose service name + its in-container port. The
		# gateway publishes 3000 to host LOOPBACK ONLY (ADR-0029), so a front is
		# the only route in from off-box.
		#
		# The gateway's requestIsSecure() reads x-forwarded-proto straight off the
		# raw headers, so the session cookie's `Secure` flag activates behind this.
		#
		# We override X-Forwarded-For with <client-ip> instead of letting Caddy
		# append: overwriting discards any client-supplied X-Forwarded-For, so the
		# left-most entry the gateway sees is always the address the front vouches
		# for and cannot be spoofed. That is what makes Fastify's `trustProxy` safe
		# to enable.
		reverse_proxy {$REGULAIT_TLS_UPSTREAM:gateway:3000} {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {args[0]}
			header_up X-Real-IP {args[0]}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {args[1]}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
			header_up -CF-Connecting-IP

			# The gateway streams SSE (playground / run output). Disable response
			# buffering so tokens reach the browser as they are produced.
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
}
